# polly — lift-source (conda-forge feedstock, plus our own build's usage)

Upstream: https://github.com/conda-forge/polly-feedstock
Fork:     https://github.com/NAGAGroup/polly-feedstock
Pinned:   6cde8e5afb3f9bf8a8d455c90b0ff246058c6649 (conda-forge main, 2026-04-22)

LIFT, NOT SUBMODULE: we do not build this feedstock. Its flags are copied into
our own core recipe, because acpp forces LLVM+clang into ONE cmake configure
and conda-forge's per-component split cannot carry it.

WHAT OUR BUILD ACTUALLY DOES, measured at main:shared/build-stage.nu:240 —
polly is an LLVM_ENABLE_PROJECTS entry on LINUX ONLY (the win branch at 338
and the mac branch at 94 omit it). Unlike bolt it ships NO named binary:
`polly` appears nowhere in main:release/recipe.yaml, so its artifacts ride the
library and header carves. That is why it needs no output of its own — but its
platform gating still belongs in a `skip:`/conditional inside a uniform output
list, not in an omission.

Watch this one: the LLVM discourse thread on building LLVM as a Windows DLL
reports a plugin symbol-resolution failure precisely in polly
(getPollyPluginInfo). It corroborates the linked-in-not-plugin ruling, and
marks a place to expect Windows trouble.

TO DIFF AGAINST UPSTREAM:
    git clone https://github.com/NAGAGroup/polly-feedstock
    git -C polly-feedstock diff 6cde8e5afb3f9bf8a8d455c90b0ff246058c6649..origin/main
